      "path": "README.md",
      "content": "# DSH-action\n\nAction v0.13.0: native project Profiles, independent network access (on by default), per-run reply history and live diagnostics. Installer changes are deferred. See [configuration](docs/native-profile.md).\n\nFor existing workflows, update the Action reference to Lixiaoyiao/DSH-action@the-existing-commit-SHA and keep its immutable commit pin. Use npm create dsh-action for the installer.\n\n[![CI](https://github.com/Lixiaoyiao/DSH-action/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/Lixiaoyiao/DSH-action/actions/workflows/ci.yml)\n[![Release](https://img.shields.io/github/v/release/Lixiaoyiao/DSH-action?display_name=tag)](https://github.com/Lixiaoyiao/DSH-action/releases/latest)\n[![MIT](https://img.shields.io/github/license/Lixiaoyiao/DSH-action)](LICENSE)\n\n[中文](README.zh-CN.md)\n\nRun DeepSeek Harness directly from GitHub pull requests, issues, failed CI jobs, and maintainer-authored automations.\n\n```text\nGitHub PR / Issue / CI  →  DeepSeek Harness  →  Review / Diagnose / Fix / Issue → PR\n```\n\nThe Action starts a credential-isolated DSH worker, validates its structured result, and lets a trusted Controller publish comments or validated changes. This is a community project, not an official DeepSeek or GitHub product. It is maintained by [@Lixiaoyiao](https://github.com/Lixiaoyiao).\n\n## Quick Start\n\nThe Action runs DSH on GitHub-hosted Linux runners. **You do not need to install DSH or Docker on your computer.** Local DSH runs on your own computer; its keys and settings are separate from the GitHub Action.\n\n### 1. Install the tools\n\nOn Windows, install [Node.js 24 or later](https://nodejs.org/en/download), [Git for Windows](https://git-scm.com/download/win), and [GitHub CLI](https://cli.github.com/). Keep the default installation options. Close and reopen PowerShell, then check:\n\n```powershell\nnode --version\nnpm.cmd --version\ngit --version\ngh --version\n```\n\nUse `npm.cmd` if PowerShell blocks `npm.ps1`; no execution-policy change is needed. On macOS and Linux, use `npm`.\n\n### 2. Open your repository and sign in\n\nUse the existing local repository folder. You do not need to clone it again.\n\n```powershell\ncd \"C:\\path\\to\\your-repository\"\ngit remote -v\ngh auth login\n```\n\nChoose GitHub.com and browser sign-in. The `origin` remote should point to the repository you want to set up. The installer uses that repository; it does not create a new one.\n\n### 3. Install the workflow and set your key\n\n```powershell\nnpm.cmd create dsh-action@latest\n```\n\nThe installer asks which workflow to install, starting with **read-only `@dsh` conversation and automatic PR Review**, then asks for the DSH composition, with `controlled` as the default. It creates local workflow files and preserves existing files. Review, commit and push the generated files with your normal Git workflow.\n\nInstaller `0.7.4` binds generated workflows to formal Action `v0.12.2`.\n\nAdd the key in **Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → New repository secret** on GitHub. Set the name to `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY` and paste the key as its value. A key stored in local DSH does not configure this secret.\n\n### 4. Ask `@dsh` a question\n\nMake sure `.github/workflows/dsh-review.yml` is on the default branch, usually `main`. If direct pushes are blocked, submit and merge a PR containing the workflow first.\n\nAs a repository collaborator with write access, add a comment in an Issue or PR:\n\n```text\n@dsh Help me understand this issue\n```\n\nYou can also use `@dsh 帮我分析这个问题`. Start `@dsh` on a line outside quotes or code; a fixed subcommand is optional. The Action replies in the thread. Requests such as `@dsh Please fix this bug` remain read-only in this starter: they produce an analysis or proposed fix without editing files. Follow progress or read failure details under **Actions → DSH review**.\n\nFor automatic review, open a **non-draft pull request** or push a new commit to an existing one. The Action reviews the PR without changing its files.\n\n### Optional: local plugins and other workflows\n\nThe terminal choices include read-only conversation + Review, Coding Commands, both, and automatic Session. `--advanced` enters these questions explicitly. If you already have local DSH plugins, `--import-plugins` lists them for selection and confirmation in the terminal:\n\n```powershell\nnpm.cmd create dsh-action@latest -- --import-plugins\n```\n\nNative mode supports DSH Bundles directly. Controlled mode also needs their exact read-only tool names (`plugin__owner__tool`). The cloud installs selected packages at exact versions; local settings and keys stay on your computer. Local-only, disabled and incompatible packages show their status. Use `--dsh-home \"C:\\path\\to\\.dsh\"` with the import option for another DSH home. Plugin import requires an interactive terminal.\n\nFor non-interactive setup, choose the workflow and composition explicitly:\n\n```powershell\nnpm.cmd create dsh-action@latest -- --mode both --dsh-mode native\n```\n\nControlled remains the default. Natural-language writes require both `natural-language-write: \"true\"` and `allow-write: \"true\"` in a write-capable workflow, repository-specific validation commands, and a clear request such as `@dsh Please fix this bug and open a PR`. The Commands starter keeps the independent natural-language switch off; legacy `fix`, `implement` and `task --write` retain their existing write requests. Installer `0.7.4` targets Action `v0.12.2` and DSH `0.2.1-rc.2`. Its build binds the qualified Action commit through `DSH_ACTION_RELEASE_SHA`; its source tag is `create-dsh-action-v0.7.4`. Edit the workflow YAML for models, thinking effort and other inputs. See the [setup guide](docs/setup.md) for configuration checks, manual setup and recovery.\n\n### Manual installation\n\nAdd `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY` under **Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions**, then create `.github/workflows/dsh-review.yml`:\n\n```yaml\nname: DSH review\n\non:\n  pull_request_target:\n    types: [opened, synchronize, ready_for_review, reopened]\n  issue_comment:\n    types: [created]\n  pull_request_review_comment:\n    types: [created]\n  pull_request_review:\n    types: [submitted]\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n  issues: write\n  pull-requests: write\n\nconcurrency:\n  group: dsh-${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request_target' && 'review' || 'chat' }}-${{ github.event.pull_request.number || github.event.issue.number }}\n  cancel-in-progress: ${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request_target' }}\n\njobs:\n  review:\n    if: >-\n      (github.event_name == 'pull_request_target' && github.event.pull_request.draft == false) ||\n      (github.event.sender.type != 'Bot' &&\n      contains(github.event.comment.body || github.event.review.body, '@dsh'))\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 30\n    steps:\n      - name: Checkout trusted base or default branch only\n        uses: actions/checkout@11d5960a326750d5838078e36cf38b85af677262 # v4\n        with:\n          ref: ${{ github.event.pull_request.base.sha || github.event.repository.default_branch }}\n          persist-credentials: false\n          fetch-depth: 1\n      - uses: Lixiaoyiao/DSH-action@v0.13.0\n        with:\n          deepseek-api-key: ${{ secrets.DEEPSEEK_API_KEY }}\n          dsh-version: 0.2.1-rc.2\n          permission-profile: strict\n          isolation: docker\n          natural-language-write: \"false\"\n          allow-write: \"false\"\n```\n\nComment `@dsh Help me understand this issue` in an Issue/PR, or open a non-draft PR for automatic review. The Action checks out only the trusted base/default branch, reads pull requests through GitHub APIs, and never executes fork code. `issues: write` and `pull-requests: write` let the Controller publish replies; code remains read-only.\n\nFor production, pin the complete commit published in the corresponding GitHub Release. Resolve the corresponding immutable release tag to its full SHA after qualification; the installer version and its Action binding are independent release identities. See [Setup](docs/setup.md) for permissions, pinning, checkout rules, and complete templates.\n\n## Core capabilities\n\n| Capability              | What it does                                                                                                     |\n| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Pull request review     | Reviews new commits, publishes one summary, and adds high-confidence inline findings                             |\n| General tasks           | Answers repository questions or performs an explicitly authorized coding task                                    |\n| CI diagnosis and repair | Reads failed checks and logs; trusted workflows may validate and publish a fix                                   |\n| Issue implementation    | Turns an authorized Issue request into a validated branch and pull request                                       |\n| Composition modes       | Keeps the existing controlled tool profile by default and offers an experimental native DSH mode                 |\n| Controlled tools        | Adds exact profiles for native, fixed-command, typed Controller GitHub, MCP, Bundle, and Plugin tools            |\n| Structured results      | Keeps the schema-v1 audit envelope and can validate an optional maintainer-defined task result                   |\n| Images and binary files | Reads PNG/JPEG/WebP/GIF through official DSH tools, downloads eligible GitHub images, and preserves binary bytes |\n| Explicit Session resume | Continues a completed task in a new Actions run using a verified raw DSH checkpoint and current permissions      |\n\nThe Action provides the experimental `dsh-mode: native` path over the locked DSH `0.2.1-rc.2` runtime and its official ecosystem composition. Native MCP servers, Profile Bundles, direct Cordis Plugins, repository Skills, Subagents, and Workflows retain DSH-native discovery and behavior. `controlled` remains the compatible default, so workflows that omit `dsh-mode` retain their existing composition, permissions, tools, budgets, receipts, and outputs.\n\nNative mode is not an unsafe mode. It returns ownership of DSH's internal headless composition, capability graph, and model-visible inventory to DSH. Native MCP servers load through official `@deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-client`; Bundles become official Profile layers; direct Plugins load through Cordis; and repository Skills, Subagents, and Workflows retain DSH-native behavior. Its definition-only extension schema declares owners and process requirements, not Action tools, grants, or per-tool budgets. Dynamic ecosystem tools appear only in runtime `observedTools`, and native `toolPolicy` never claims Controller `effectiveTools`.\n\nThe Action still owns trusted-workflow admission, exact package pins, lifecycle-script suppression, runtime inventory audit, Docker and `.git`-less workspace boundaries, the run-scoped DeepSeek credential proxy, GitHub credential isolation, actor/repository trust, validation and deferred writes, deadlines, cancellation, and secret redaction. Native remains Docker-only. Bridge network and read/write mounts are whole-worker capabilities, not per-extension or per-tool sandboxes. A user-configured GitHub MCP with its own credential is a trusted external extension whose direct effects do not receive the Controller Gateway's binding, revalidation, validation, or deferred-mutation guarantees. Controller-owned `command.*` and `github.*` capabilities remain a separate, mode-independent plane.\n\nThe Action supports [automatic Session creation and continuation by key](docs/session.md), retaining explicit save/resume. A trusted workflow discovers the latest successful compatible checkpoint; failed, unknown, missing, expired or corrupt history prevents silent fallback. Matching workflow identity and concurrency bindings serialize one key while allowing different keys to run concurrently. The existing Controller / DSH / Gateway / validation ownership, exact `0.2.1-rc.2` pin and default-off Session path remain. Current authority is recalculated; previous GitHub writes are not replayed. [Text file context](docs/text-files.md), [offline configuration checks](docs/configuration-check.md) and [bounded GitHub diagnostics](docs/github-requests.md) remain available.\n\n### Images and files\n\nUpload a PNG, JPG, WebP or GIF to a GitHub Issue, PR or comment and ask `@dsh task --read` about it. In an eligible trusted Docker run with `workspace.read`, the Controller downloads GitHub-hosted images automatically and DSH's official `read_image` reads them. Repository images use the same tool. Downloads allow 8 image attempts, 4 MiB each and 16 MiB total; skipped or failed images produce a reason in the run logs. Untrusted PRs retain their existing tool permissions.\n\nBinary repository files retain their exact bytes through workspace copies and Git Blob publication. Creating or changing them requires an already authorized binary-capable command or extension and the existing write validation. Session checkpoints also carry the bytes referenced by official image/file attachments. PDF, Word and Excel remain opaque files unless a maintainer configures a suitable DSH extension; the Action does not include a document parser. See [file support and limits](docs/file-support.md).\n\n## Live runs\n\nThese public runs show the comments and Actions logs produced by this repository.\n\n| Scenario                                                | Run                                                                                                                                         |\n| ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| PR review, including a rerun without duplicate comments | [PR #3](https://github.com/Lixiaoyiao/DSH-action/pull/3) · [Actions run](https://github.com/Lixiaoyiao/DSH-action/actions/runs/31760570162) |\n| Diagnosis based on failed checks and logs               | [Actions run](https://github.com/Lixiaoyiao/DSH-action/actions/runs/31760603284)                                                            |\n| Fix and validation in trusted write mode                | [Actions run](https://github.com/Lixiaoyiao/DSH-action/actions/runs/31761793492)                                                            |\n| Issue implementation followed by a pull request         | [Issue #4](https://github.com/Lixiaoyiao/DSH-action/issues/4) → [PR #5](https://github.com/Lixiaoyiao/DSH-action/pull/5)                    |\n\n## Natural requests and existing `@dsh` commands\n\nWrite `@dsh` followed by your request on a non-code line of an Issue or PR comment, PR review, or inline review comment. It may follow context or blank lines. Use one actionable mention per comment. Quoted examples, code blocks, inline code and bot-authored natural requests do not trigger a task. Mid-sentence mentions such as `Please ask @dsh about this` are ignored.\n\n| Command                                  | Purpose                                                                                            |\n| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `@dsh Explain this failure`              | Run a general task; read-only unless the workflow explicitly enables write tasks                   |\n| `@dsh Please fix this bug and open a PR` | Request a write task only when the workflow enables natural-language writes and normal write gates |\n| `@dsh task --read <question>`            | Explain code, inspect the repository, or answer a general question                                 |\n| `@dsh task --write <task>`               | Request a coding task; every write gate must still pass                                            |\n| `@dsh review`                            | Review the current pull request again                                                              |\n| `@dsh diagnose`                          | Diagnose failed checks and logs                                                                    |\n| `@dsh fix`                               | Repair a same-repository pull request in trusted write mode                                        |\n| `@dsh implement`                         | Implement an Issue and open a pull request                                                         |\n\nThe first words `task`, `review`, `diagnose`, `fix` and `implement` retain their existing command semantics, case-insensitively. For example, `@dsh fix` is an explicit write request even when natural-language writes are disabled; the read-only starter denies it. Use `@dsh Please fix this bug` for a natural request.\n\n`--write`, `fix`, and `implement` request capabilities; they do not grant them. The workflow must explicitly enable write mode and provide Controller-run validation. Natural requests are read-only unless `natural-language-write` is enabled and the request clearly asks to change code. `task-access` does not enable natural-language writes. See [Usage](docs/usage.md) for commands and automation, and [Configuration](docs/configuration.md) for the gates.\n\nMaintainers can change the trigger phrase, add label/assignee routes, filter actors or historical comments, select a base branch, and choose a deterministic branch template. These settings change routing and naming only; GitHub authority still comes from the Controller policy and workflow token scopes.\n\n## Security\n\n- The Agent receives neither the real `GITHUB_TOKEN` nor the real DeepSeek key. Only the Controller can call Action-owned `github.*` mutation APIs; an explicitly configured external GitHub MCP uses its own credential and authority as described below.\n- Repository content, diffs, issues, pull requests, comments, logs, model output, and tool output remain untrusted data.\n- Fork review uses a `.git`-less, credential-free worker and must check out only the trusted base SHA with `persist-credentials: false`.\n- Writes require a trusted same-repository context, authorized actors, Docker, `allow-write: \"true\"`, non-empty fixed validation commands, and successful validation. Protected-path and Validation Integrity checks still apply.\n- Typed `github.*` mutations are exact-ID, entity-bound, deferred until Controller validation, and reconciled with bounded receipts. No arbitrary REST, GraphQL, URL, or credential pass-through exists.\n- Validation Integrity provides high-confidence weakening detection plus baseline replay for its supported entrypoints, scripts, test/config weakening, lock/toolchain controls, and known wrappers/interpreters; it is not complete cross-language dependency provenance or a formal proof.\n- Validation may use Docker bridge networking. On self-hosted or corporate-network runners, repository validation code may reach runner-accessible network services; use dedicated runners and runner-level segmentation/egress controls.\n- An approved Bundle, Plugin, or stdio MCP server is trusted worker code. Controlled ToolRuntime limits model-routed calls; native leaves routing and inventory to DSH. Neither model is a sandbox for extension startup, background work, or direct process I/O, and any bridge/RW capability applies to the whole worker.\n- `github.*` uses the Controller GitHub Gateway. A separately configured GitHub MCP uses its own external credential and is outside the Gateway's binding, revalidation, validation, and deferred-write guarantees.\n\n`worker-network` is the Worker public-egress switch: default `true`, explicit `false` for isolation. Plugin network flags do not control it. Read the [Security policy](SECURITY.md) when choosing network policy, write mode, host execution or third-party extensions.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Guide                                                                         | Contents                                                                             |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| [Setup](docs/setup.md) · [中文](docs/setup.zh-CN.md)                          | Installer, manual setup, Secret, permissions, safe checkout, and templates           |\n| [Usage](docs/usage.md) · [中文](docs/usage.zh-CN.md)                          | `@dsh` commands, tasks, review, diagnose, fix, implement, and automation             |\n| [Configuration](docs/configuration.md)                                        | Inputs, permission profiles, tools, validation, extensions, and outputs              |\n| [Files and images](docs/file-support.md) · [中文](docs/zh-CN/file-support.md) | Supported types, GitHub downloads, binary bytes, Session attachments, and limits     |\n| [Architecture](ARCHITECTURE.md) · [Invariants](docs/invariants.md)            | Component ownership, authority boundaries, and stable system invariants              |\n| [Troubleshooting](docs/troubleshooting.md)                                    | Denials, Docker, timeouts, cancellation, validation, and extension failures          |\n| [Security policy](SECURITY.md)                                                | Trust model, credential boundaries, network behavior, and known limitations          |\n| [Extension contracts](docs/extension-contracts.md)                            | Deep technical contracts for MCP, Profile, Bundle, Plugin, ToolRuntime, and receipts |\n| [Maintainer release guide](docs/maintainer-release.md)                        | Local checks, Core E2E, release canary, version updates, and publishing              |\n| [Contributing](CONTRIBUTING.md) · [Changelog](CHANGELOG.md)                   | Development workflow and release history                                             |\n\n## Development\n\nNode.js 24 is required.\n\n```bash\nnpm ci\nnpm run check\n```\n\nSee [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md). The Marketplace `dist/` bundle is committed for releases and must not be edited by hand.\n\n## License\n\n[MIT](LICENSE). Third-party licenses are listed in [THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md](THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md) and [BUNDLED_DEPENDENCIES.md](BUNDLED_DEPENDENCIES.md).\n\nDeepSeek Harness supplies the headless runtime and official extension mechanisms. The GitHub integration also draws on the MIT-licensed [Claude Code Action](https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action) patterns and the execution/publication separation described by [Codex GitHub Action](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/github-action); exact attributions are recorded in the third-party notices.\n"
